Rule Thoughts (@rule-thoughts: 0) {
# Condition
- [ ] **Read a number out of a namespaced Record extension**, so a rule can use a Record's weight, price and count and not only its quantity. A missing one is an error, never a silent zero. Dunno if this is better to have only when json like extension so we can query properties like @myrecord.extension.weight that queries the json, or if @myrecord.extension that would have to have some 'return some-quantitative-value'.

# Threshold
- [ ] This can be one thing:  **Debounce:** a reading must hold for a declared span before it counts, so a value flickering across the line does not fire ten times. **Cooldown:** after firing, refuse for a declared span. **Rate limit:** at most `max` firings in a rolling window. These three are temporal controls on the Threshold, not new rule kinds, and each needs its state persisted so a restart does not forget a cooldown.

# Consequence
- [x] **Cycles are discovered at proof time**, not at runtime.
      - [ ] After interface refactor the karma sand should give the user the notice about the cycle when they eventually create one. **Cycle detection needs more than the UI notice.** Proof checks cycles _inside a Program_. I did not find equivalent checks across Rules in the editor’s save path; rule reactions still have a runtime cutoff. I would add: “Check chains across Rules before saving, and show which Records and Rules form the cycle.” Program proof (crates/nucleus/src/karma/proof.rs:930), Rule validation (crates/engine/src/rule\_runtime.rs:171). This can be part of Simulation's job, i want it that way. And these cycles can be daily (like every day at 13h it's -1, at 00h if -1 becomes 0) not necessairily instant (two rules continuously firing to change to another value, with the change triggering the other rule's evaluation).

- [ ] Instead of Commands immediatelly being the calling of some bash and putting it's value in some Condition we save it as a Signal, only then it is used in the Conditon. We decouple the receiving of data with it's usage.
- [ ] Being able to accept commands over some endpoint, with some form of access control. so /command/{slug or id} is not callable by anyone.
- [ ] **Command → Signal → Condition already exists in the backend.** Commands produce saved Signal samples, and rules react to those samples. That checkbox should distinguish the existing backend from any remaining authoring/interface work. Existing test (crates/engine/tests/rule\_runtime.rs:389)

- [ ] Karma Frequency could schedules the returning of the bike after 3 days be a proposed contribution from my side. Same thing for the scheduling of my need of a bike for 3 days later.

- [ ] **Temporal controls are partly built.** Debounce, cooldown, and rate limits already exist for Karma Programs; persisted cooldown even has a restart test. The remaining work is connecting these controls to the ordinary Rule Threshold and its editor. Controls (crates/nucleus/src/karma/ast.rs:111), restart test (crates/store/tests/karma\_runs.rs:344)

- [ ] **Simulate rules before accepting changes:** try scenarios against the user’s data, share proposed changes, enforce constraints, and show future quantities in a calendar. Simulation (institute/anicca/Tasks.lingua:112). I put some tasks to transfer that it should have in the simulation castle some way to add some premade checks/constraints so that some values never reach some state, or dont reach them in the end. I'd like to be able to apply that to karma too, so i can set my constraints and when i change karma with simulation on it is run in the backend and it returns me some report if some constraint was broken and some statistics like for this period of simulation this value was median, average, mode, and final.

- [ ] **Let Fiote create and edit rules:** suggest habit changes, simulate proposals, and explain proposals rejected by constraints. Fiote (institute/anicca/Tasks.lingua:145). Yes give it ability to crud karma and it's tables and talk to me about them.

- [ ] **Make board interactions usable by Karma:** moving Sands can trigger rules; consequences can move, show, or hide Sands. Board interactions (institute/anicca/Tasks.lingua:165). This needs more refinement, i was thinking of adding castles/sands to some area that i will see, like a notification, that starts pinned somewhere in a part of my screen, so many things can spawn and they flex wrap inside an area of sorting. Maybe this can be a karma affected protein spawning area, like karma mail. So it can spawn at time x some castle by code, with some state, like being a record of a call with someone, so i automatically enter a call with some relative at some point. Or it can be to open another protein+sorting area that gives me record titles that are negative quantity so it's a list of task names that i can click a checkbox button to bring them to zero. I'd like those workflows to happen. What do you think, let's refine what is written to a karma consequence to spawn those sands/castles/areas and b

- [ ] **Automate Transfers:** propose purchases, donations, and returns when a quantity or date calls for them. Transfer automation (institute/anicca/Lince.lingua:565), scheduled returns (institute/anicca/Tasks.lingua:266). This needs lots of thought.

- [ ] **Provide reusable Karma Trails:** workflows for habits, supplies, and recurring obligations that people connect to their own Records. Karma Trails (institute/anicca/Lince.lingua:529). Sure, lets enhance the karmas in the trails/habits/simple/ and add that to the set of .lingua that gets ingested into Instinct, when the person is reading the karma part of instinct there is a button to dump the ingested frequency+rules+records into their db. I'm thinking of doing that myself, i'll fill it with good habits later, just to the wiring and the hello world of a daily Cleaning Room recurring task.


On your numbered points: 1. agree. 2. make sure this is simple, like cooldown and rate limit are the same, debounce can be also the same??

1. **Extension readings: choose a namespaced property.**

   I recommend reading a number from an explicitly named extension and property. `@myrecord.extension.weight` leaves the namespace unclear; the exact syntax can come later.

   An extension that “returns a value” introduces execution into reading data. Calculations can stay in Conditions; commands can produce Signals.

   Suggested wording: “Read numeric properties from namespaced Record extensions. Missing or nonnumeric values stop that evaluation with an explanation. Changes to a watched property wake its Rules, even when the Record’s quantity stays unchanged.”
2. **Temporal controls: one place to configure them, three different behaviors.**

   Debounce, cooldown, and rate limit should share the Threshold editor, but remain separately configurable. Merge the later “partly built” entry into this task.

   First define whether a Rule fires on every qualifying update or only when its Threshold starts passing. Otherwise, ten identical Signal samples might mean ten actions—or one—and neither interpretation is obvious.

   I suggest debounce means “the Threshold keeps passing for this duration,” rather than requiring an unchanged number. Its deadline must wake the Rule without waiting for another update. Cooldown and rate limits should suppress firings; a Frequency should control repetition.

   Save timing state **per Rule**, even when Rules share a Threshold definition. Count a firing when its action is durably accepted; retries belong to that same firing.
3. **Cycles: your daily example should be allowed.**

   My earlier suggestion to check chains before saving was too broad if interpreted as rejecting every cycle. A daily reset is normal recurrence. Even an immediate chain can return to an earlier Record and then stop.

   Simulation can own the analysis and show the chain. However, a finite simulation cannot establish that every future execution will terminate.

   Suggested wording: “Use Simulation to explain repeating Rule chains and find reactions that keep running without time advancing. Allow intended recurrence. Report the period and inputs checked, and distinguish a found loop from a run that reached its work limit.”

   Keep a runtime limit as well. The existing 256-evaluation cutoff (crates/engine/src/rule\_runtime.rs:262) can also stop a long valid chain, so reaching it should not itself be reported as proof of a cycle.
4. **Signals and incoming requests: remove the duplicate task and define failures.**

   The command-to-Signal backend exists. The remaining task should cover creating, configuring, inspecting, and using Signals.

   A Signal needs a last successful reading, its time, and the latest failure. Decide how old a reading may be before a Condition refuses it. Currently, sampling updates the timestamp before checking success (crates/engine/src/effects.rs:189), so that timestamp alone cannot establish freshness.

   “Accept commands over an endpoint” also contains two different operations: submit a Signal sample, or request execution of a saved command. Define which the endpoint permits, authorize access to that specific target, and make repeated delivery of one request produce one result.
5. **The bicycle example needs an agreed due date.**

   “After three days” needs a starting event. I would use the agreed return date attached to the borrowing Transfer.

   Suggested wording: “At the agreed return date, create a return proposal and update the expected Need. Rescheduling or cancelling the borrowing updates the scheduled action. Repeating its delivery must not create another proposal.”

   The scheduled date should not itself declare that the physical return happened.
6. **Simulation: distinguish checking a scenario from enforcing a live constraint.**

   “Never below zero during this run” and “at least zero at the end” are useful, different checks. Reuse the existing Simulation checks (crates/desktop/src/simulation\_castle/checks\_ui.rs:1).

   With Simulation enabled, evaluate the proposed Rule change before activating it. Show the first broken constraint, its time, and the actions leading to it. An incomplete run must remain visibly incomplete.

   For quantities, average, median, and mode should account for **time spent at each value**. Counting updates would make a frequently updated Record distort the statistics. Include minimum, maximum, and final value too.

   Future Signal readings need declared assumptions or supplied samples. Simulated calls, commands, and Transfers should appear as proposed effects without performing the real action.
7. **Fiote: describe its abilities through ordinary actions.**

   I would replace “CRUD karma and its tables” with: “Fiote can inspect, create, edit, pause, and remove Rules, Frequencies, and Signals through the same actions and permissions as the interface.”

   Add explaining why a Rule fired or did not fire, and running the same Simulation checks on a proposal. Direct database-table editing would bypass the behavior you are trying to make consistent.
8. **Board workflows: make the consequence a saved presentation request.**

   Your pinned Protein area is a good fit. A Rule could request: “Show this saved Castle or area template here, with these Records and settings.” The interface then renders it using existing placement and sorting.

   The request needs a destination, a template, supplied data, and a lifetime. One firing should produce one item; reconnecting should not duplicate it. Dismissing an item and completing its task should remain separate actions.

   For the task-list example, a permanently placed Protein area filtering negative quantities may already provide the desired behavior. Karma only needs to change the underlying Records.

   For calls, showing a call Castle and initiating a call should be separately configurable consequences. Automatic calling can then specify the device and existing call permissions without making “display this Castle” unexpectedly start communication.
9. **Transfer automation: split it into concrete capabilities.**

   Start with creating or updating a draft proposal from a Rule. Publishing, accepting, and fulfilling a Transfer are separate actions with their own authority and conditions.

   A persistent shortage must not create another purchase proposal on every evaluation. Define whether the Rule updates an outstanding proposal or waits for it to finish. The bicycle return is a useful first example because it has a clear origin and endpoint.
10. **The Cleaning Room Trail is a suitable first complete workflow.**

I would specify: at the chosen local time each day, set Cleaning Room to `-1`; checking it off sets it to `0`. Setting `-1` avoids accumulating a larger debt for missed days.

The Instinct button should explain what it imports, handle name conflicts, and avoid duplicating or resetting the example when clicked again. Importing today should not replay years of missed cleaning tasks.

This tests Frequencies, Rules, task presentation, completion, and importing together before you add more habits.

The `.ics` task fits in Blood (institute/anicca/Lince.lingua:334). I would initially scope it to exporting the scheduled or projected occurrences already shown in the calendar.

}

